= Appendix

== Additional result: S2E models rank MPRA variants best in promoters and terminators, worst in 5′ UTRs
*Motivation* Massively parallel reporter assays (MPRAs) screen thousands to millions of DNA sequences at one specific position with a fixed reporter protein. Models that have learned the appropriate regulatory logic of gene expression in those regions should be able to rank the different sequences correctly.

*Task* Here, we examine four different datasets, each probing a different part of regulatory logic and benchmarked in the same way. We predict expression for a sequence construct $i$ and compute gene expression as the sum of the coding-sequence bins. This is then correlated with the true fluorescence measurements. The datasets investigate promoter, 5' untranslated region, coding sequence, and terminator variants.

*Data* We use four different datasets from @rafi_community_2024, @cuperus_deep_2017, @chen_codon-resolution_2017, and @shalem_systematic_2015. _Rafi et al._ @rafi_community_2024 provide a dataset of approximately 70,000 80-bp promoter sequences, each embedded in a plasmid-based promoter scaffold that drives the transcription of a fluorescent protein. Cells are sorted into 18 bins by fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). The cell-count-weighted mean bin is z-normalised and then reported as a proxy for _reporter expression_. _Cuperus et al._ @cuperus_deep_2017 engineer 500,000 5'-untranslated-region (5' UTR) variants and measure gene expression in a pooled growth assay. We note that variants in the 5' UTR also alter translation efficiency, which no model currently captures. However, variants can, for example, alter mRNA stability, which is within the theoretical capabilities of current models. _Chen et al._ @chen_codon-resolution_2017 perturb small stretches (up to 20 bp) of the coding sequences of two genes, GFP and TDH3, while keeping their amino acid sequences unchanged. Their proxy for gene expression is the ratio of RNA-seq reads to DNA-seq reads for each variant in the pool. _Shalem et al._ @shalem_systematic_2015 screen a collection of thousands of terminator variants in a similar manner to @rafi_community_2024. Terminator variants are known to alter transcription stop sites and transcript isoform distributions, resulting, for example, in different mRNA stabilities.

*Metrics* Suppose $V$ is a set of variable sequences for a given dataset. We compute predictions $hat(Y)$ for a set of constructs $C$. Each construct is a concatenation of the _fixed upstream sequence_, _variable sequence v_, and _fixed downstream sequence_. Depending on the dataset, the reporter protein is located in either the upstream or downstream sequence, or both.
$
C = {"concat"("upstream_fixed", v, "downstream_fixed") | v in V}
$
$
hat(Y) = {f_(i:j)(c) | c in C}
$
where $i$ and $j$ are the start and end positions of the reporter gene's coding sequence. Given the set $Y$ of experimentally measured reporter-expression values, we report Spearman $rho$ and Pearson $r$ for ${(y_c, hat(y)_c) | c in C}$. Both Yorzoi and Shorkie are evaluated zero-shot, i.e., they have not been trained on the respective assay data.

*Results* Broadly speaking, we observe the strongest correlations with experimentally measured gene expression for the promoter and terminator assays ($r$ and $rho$ around 0.7 ± 0.07), slightly weaker correlations for synonymous codon sequences, and the weakest for the 5' UTR MPRA. _Shorkie_ outperforms _Yorzoi_ on the promoter MPRA. We speculate that this is mostly attributable to its masked-language-modelling pretraining procedure, since promoter motifs are well conserved across yeast species. To contextualise the result, we also report the performance of a supervised baseline, _DREAM-RNN_ @rafi_community_2024, evaluated on the same test set but trained on a much larger set of sequences from the same experiment. This baseline demonstrates a substantial margin over both zero-shot approaches ($Delta r=$0.21 compared with Shorkie).

In contrast, terminator motifs are more cryptic and less conserved. We speculate that _Yorzoi's_ advantage over _Shorkie_ on the terminator MPRA stems from its training on genomically rearranged sequences, in which some coding sequences are seen multiple times with different terminator sequences. When evaluating synonymous coding sequences, we chose to add two zero-shot models that use only the coding sequence to predict gene expression: _CodonTransformer_ @fallahpour_codontransformer_2025 and the _Codon Adaptation Index_ (CAI), with the latter being a simple non-parametric formula. Surprisingly, CAI and CodonTransformer outperform Shorkie and Yorzoi on two of the three subtasks (GFP region 2 and TDH3), while Yorzoi and Shorkie have the best average performance.

The _Cuperus et al._ @cuperus_deep_2017 assay is the least predictable. We suspect that even perfect RNA-seq coverage prediction would not yield a very high correlation in this assay because the 5' UTR sequence, especially around the start codon, has a major influence on translation efficiency, which current models do not capture directly. A second reason lies in the assay itself: a pooled growth experiment in HIS3-knockout strains, for which it is unclear whether enrichment in the pool remains correlated with protein expression above a certain protein abundance. Histidine biosynthesis is known to be product-inhibited; i.e., overexpression of HIS3 increases histidine synthesis only up to a certain point. Future models that predict protein abundance in addition to transcript abundance might improve upon the results shown here.

